<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacqueline, Countess of Hainaut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1401">1401</a>–<a href="/years/1436">1436</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jacqueline of Bavaria, Jacoba of Bavaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacqueline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, inherited Hainaut, Holland and Zeeland in 1417. Her claims were contested by her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-bavaria">John of Bavaria</a> and her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in a long war. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-delft">Treaty of Delft</a> in 1428 Philip became her heir, and in 1433 she gave up her counties to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacqueline-countess-of-hainaut.jpg" alt="Jacqueline, Countess of Hainaut" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_van_Eyck_097.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
